<% content_for :title, "Turf Score Benchmarks — #{@slate.name}" %>

<%# The public pricing board. THE PRICE is read, never recomputed: rank and
    Turf Score come off the stored columns settlement multiplies by
    (Slate#team_rows), and a page that recomputed them would eventually
    disagree with what a player is paid — the failure that column exists to
    prevent.

    Other numbers here ARE derived live, and saying so plainly is the point of
    this note: `game_factor` and `expected_points_per_game` are computed from
    the matchups on each render, and the chart's two lines are computed from
    `SlateMatchup.turf_score_for` per rank. None of those is money — they
    describe the team and the rule, not the payout — and deriving them is what
    keeps the picture honest when the rule or the slate changes. The dots are
    the exception that proves it: they plot the STORED price, which is why a
    hand-edited multiplier shows up as a dot off its line. %>
<%# Chart tokens — the two SERIES colours only. Everything else in the chart
    (axis text, gridlines, labels) inherits `currentColor` from the app's own
    text utilities, and the dot rings use --color-surface, so the chart follows
    the theme without this file inventing token names the design system does
    not actually define.

    DARK IS ITS OWN STEP, not an automatic flip: the light pair
    sits at OKLCH L .675 which is outside the dark band, so dark gets slightly
    deeper steps of the same two hues. Both pairs were validated for
    colour-vision separation against their own surface (deutan ΔE 26.1, normal
    ΔE 32.1) rather than eyeballed. %>
<style>
  [data-testid="benchmarks-chart"] {
    --bench-full: #8E82FE;
    --bench-bye: #4BAF50;
  }
  .dark [data-testid="benchmarks-chart"] {
    --bench-full: #8577F8;
    --bench-bye: #48A84E;
  }
</style>

<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto py-8 px-4" data-testid="benchmarks">
  <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-heading mb-2">Turf Score Benchmarks</h1>
  <p class="text-secondary text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
    What the betting market expects every team to score, and the multiplier that earns. Teams are ranked on projected points
    <span class="font-semibold">per game</span>; your points are <span class="text-heading font-semibold">points scored &times; Turf Score</span>.
    <%= link_to "How Turf Score works", turf_monster_v1_path, class: "text-primary hover:underline" %>.
  </p>

  <%# Slate pills, public twins of the admin selector — same ordering, so the
      span a contest is on sits next to the weeks it holds. %>
  <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6">
    <% @slates.each do |slate| %>
      <% active = slate.id == @slate.id %>
      <%= link_to "#{slate.sport_emoji} #{slate.selector_label}", benchmarks_path(slug: slate.slug),
            class: "px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full text-sm font-semibold whitespace-nowrap transition-all #{active ? 'bg-primary text-white' : 'bg-surface-alt text-secondary hover:text-heading'}" %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <div class="card p-5 mb-6">
    <p class="text-heading font-semibold"><%= @slate.name %></p>
    <p class="text-secondary text-sm mt-1" data-testid="benchmarks-source">
      <% if @snapshot %>
        <% derived = @snapshots.sum(&:derived_count) %>
        <% rows = @snapshots.sum(&:row_count) %>
        Lines from DraftKings, read through ESPN&rsquo;s public scoreboard, pulled
        <%= @snapshot.captured_at.in_time_zone("America/New_York").strftime("%b %-d, %Y at %-l:%M %p %Z") %>.
        <%= derived == rows ? "Every team" : "#{derived} of #{rows} team" %> total<%= "s" unless derived == rows %>
        derived from the game total and the spread.
      <% else %>
        Lines have not been recorded for these weeks yet.
      <% end %>
    </p>
    <% if @slate.two_line_pricing? %>
      <p class="text-secondary text-sm mt-3" data-testid="benchmarks-two-line">
        <span class="font-semibold text-heading">Two lines.</span>
        A team with its bye inside these weeks plays <%= @slate.games_per_team - 1 %> games, not <%= @slate.games_per_team %>.
        It is ranked on points per game with everyone else, then priced on the bye line &mdash; the same curve
        <span class="font-mono text-violet">&times; <%= number_with_precision(Slate.game_factor(@slate.games_per_team, @slate.games_per_team - 1), precision: 1) %></span>
        &mdash; so a bye neither helps nor hurts a pick.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%= render "two_line_chart", slate: @slate, team_rows: @team_rows %>

  <div class="card overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="text-muted text-xs uppercase tracking-wider">
          <th class="px-3 py-3 text-left font-semibold">#</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-3 text-left font-semibold">Team</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-3 text-right font-semibold">Games</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-3 text-right font-semibold">Proj / game</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-3 text-right font-semibold">Proj total</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-3 text-right font-semibold">Turf Score</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% @team_rows.each do |row| %>
          <tr class="border-t border-subtle">
            <td class="px-3 py-3 font-mono text-muted"><%= row.rank || "—" %></td>
            <td class="px-3 py-3">
              <span class="text-heading font-semibold"><%= row.team.emoji %> <%= row.team.name %></span>
              <% if row.game_factor > 1 %>
                <span class="ml-1 text-xs font-semibold text-violet whitespace-nowrap" data-testid="benchmarks-bye-badge">bye line</span>
              <% end %>
            </td>
            <td class="px-3 py-3 text-right font-mono text-secondary"><%= row.matchups.size %></td>
            <td class="px-3 py-3 text-right font-mono text-secondary"><%= sprintf("%.1f", row.expected_points_per_game) %></td>
            <td class="px-3 py-3 text-right font-mono text-muted"><%= sprintf("%.1f", row.expected_points) %></td>
            <td class="px-3 py-3 text-right font-mono font-bold text-violet">
              <%# One decimal, like the rules page's curve cards: down a column,
                  "1.5x / 2x" reads as two unrelated numbers where "1.5x / 2.0x"
                  reads as a scale. %>
              <%= row.turf_score ? "#{sprintf('%.1f', row.turf_score)}x" : "—" %>
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="text-muted text-xs mt-4">
    Projections are the market&rsquo;s, not ours: each team&rsquo;s share of its game&rsquo;s total, split by the spread. A multiplier is frozen
    when a slate is priced &mdash; the number you see when you pick is the number you are paid.
  </p>
</div>
